    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-213229",
      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14121140/assets/9cddd6584fee52757b1c7a470a6eeffee3678d569e36025ca70c99e5543c71c2.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent a relation, defined on the set $A={\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\delta },$ that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive?",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be neither reflective nor irreflexive, some of the points $(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),$ $(\\beta ,\\beta ),$ and $(\\delta ,\\delta )$ must be in $R,$ and others must not.\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14121140/assets/7d4257c9e249f9a4c2a53c5566292e21ae6dfa7f0e08e27042548da2d8e7e000.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to a relation that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive must contain some diagonal points, but not all of them. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
